WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Hey guys, I’m Ola.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.480
I built Clause for Sandstone.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.480
Let’s jump in.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.080
Here’s the workspace.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.720
I can see what’s waiting on legal, open a request, and get to the document from one place.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.490
This card is our request.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:21.130
Acme wants a one year disclosure term, while keeping the exceptions and five year survival period.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.797
We also need to update the governing law.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:28.650
I’ve put the outcome beside the document so I can keep checking what we’re trying to achieve.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:31.410
I’ll open the agreement from the request.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:39.623
The board gives the work some context, but the heart of the assessment is right here: edit document text and search its content.

00:00:39.677 --> 00:00:43.010
Let’s start by finding the clause we actually need to change.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.197
I’m searching for the two year disclosure term.

00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:49.957
The result gives me the matching passage and the document version.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:53.490
When I open it, Clause selects that exact text.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:57.770
I don’t have to hunt through the agreement or guess which occurrence the result meant.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.930
Now I’ll replace two years with one year.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.930
I’m staging the edit first, so the saved document hasn’t changed.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:09.303
The engine checks that this target exists and identifies one place.

00:01:09.357 --> 00:01:14.130
If the wording repeats, I need context or an occurrence number to make it precise.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.850
Here’s the redline.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.317
Red is what I’m removing, and green is what I’m adding.

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:21.170
We’re still on version one.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:28.130
Every edit in a batch refers to that same original text, so an earlier replacement can’t move a later target.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.690
If one edit is invalid, none of the batch saves.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:37.580
Now, Clause CI is a check against requirements I’ve saved for this document.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:44.860
Here I want to keep the disclosure exception, retain the five year survival language, and avoid a perpetual term.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:48.780
These are exact text checks, so they don’t need a model call.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.620
Let me deliberately make a bad change.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.953
I’m staging removal of the disclosure exception.

00:01:55.007 --> 00:01:58.687
The sentence is there, so the edit itself is possible.

00:01:58.713 --> 00:02:01.500
But it’s language we said we wanted to keep.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.900
Nothing has been saved yet, and that’s the important part.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.433
When I run the check, you can see the exception requirement fail against the proposed text.

00:02:10.487 --> 00:02:12.500
The other rules still hold.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.673
This is why I separate finding the right text from checking the result.

00:02:16.727 --> 00:02:20.860
An edit can be precise and still break the thing I wanted to preserve.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.500
I’ll discard that mistaken removal and check again.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:29.580
Now the one year change is still staged, and the protected language stays intact.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.847
Exact checks are repeatable checks on text.

00:02:32.873 --> 00:02:37.780
When I ask a model to judge meaning, I treat its answer as advice that can be wrong.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.540
That looks right, so I’ll apply it.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.380
We now have version two.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.620
The saved text, search index, and history update together.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.740
I still decide whether the legal request is complete.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:54.060
Applying an edit doesn’t automatically mean the business outcome has been met.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.270
Next, I’ll use the assistant for the governing law change.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:04.110
I’m asking for Delaware to become New York while keeping those exceptions and the survival period.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.190
It’s the same fictional agreement, and I’m asking for a proposal I can inspect.

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.377
This part uses a real model request.

00:03:11.403 --> 00:03:15.150
The model suggests edits; it can’t save them directly.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.630
The usual editing engine checks the targets afterward.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:22.670
I’ve bounded the request to twenty five seconds and limited the output.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.550
An oversized replacement gets rejected rather than cut off.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.710
Here’s the proposal, its explanation, and the version it was checked against.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.470
I can review it before I apply anything.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:41.550
Semantic checks also verify quoted evidence, but a real quote can still be used to support a wrong claim.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.950
I don’t treat that as legal approval.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:48.070
There’s also an optional experimental judge called Jev.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:52.470
My existing account returned an access error, so it’s disabled here.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.550
I haven’t measured its accuracy.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:01.590
The separate language model citation experiment includes false support cases, which is a useful reminder of the limits.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.213
I’m happy with this proposed change, so I’ll apply it and create version three.

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:10.253
The server checks the version again at save time.

00:04:10.307 --> 00:04:14.173
If someone else got there first, my stale edit would be rejected.

00:04:14.227 --> 00:04:17.160
It won’t silently overwrite their newer work.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.760
In Activity, I can see the revisions and the changes behind them.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.640
Restoring makes another version instead of erasing history.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.640
The Word export includes tracked changes.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:35.200
I tested accepting and rejecting those with independent readers; I’m not claiming every Word version was checked.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.907
Let’s search for the language I just saved.

00:04:37.933 --> 00:04:40.013
It’s available straight away.

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:46.093
The server returns a consistent search snapshot, and the browser checks the loaded version and passage.

00:04:46.147 --> 00:04:49.453
If a hit is stale, it asks me to search again.

00:04:49.507 --> 00:04:52.840
It also protects any edits I already have staged.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.010
I’ve also added source-backed templates.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.610
The details show the source, version, license, and attribution.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:04.623
Common Paper provenance follows a document created from a template.

00:05:04.677 --> 00:05:08.997
The curated form stays read only, while the new copy is editable.

00:05:09.023 --> 00:05:11.530
It’s a starting point that still needs review.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.250
These integrations are a demo with sample files.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:19.063
They show how browsing and importing a document would feel.

00:05:19.117 --> 00:05:22.250
They aren’t live customer repository connections.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:28.930
Real connections need the provider credentials and workspace admin setup, and I’ve kept that distinction visible.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.170
On mobile, search gets the full screen.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:38.143
That gives the passage and results room to breathe instead of squeezing them into a desktop dialog.

00:05:38.197 --> 00:05:47.690
The browser journeys cover mobile and desktop, including exact selection, protected drafts, and older responses arriving after a later navigation.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:51.100
Let me show you the API behind that edit.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.487
I send JSON with the version I expect, the text I’m targeting, and its replacement.

00:05:56.513 --> 00:05:59.940
The response gives me updated text and a change receipt.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:03.020
This calls the same editing service the screen uses.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:07.620
And here’s a real stale version response: a conflict.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:12.367
I asked to edit version one, but the document was already on version three.

00:06:12.393 --> 00:06:14.740
The current text stays safe.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.380
Ambiguous targets and overlapping changes fail too.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:22.420
Offset edits must include the text expected at that position.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.860
The stack is fairly direct.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.100
React handles the interface.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.580
Fastify validates requests.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.913
A pure TypeScript engine resolves the changes.

00:06:32.967 --> 00:06:36.580
The database commits only if the version still matches.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.153
SQLite runs locally, and Neon Postgres runs the hosted app.

00:06:41.207 --> 00:06:43.340
Their indexes serve search.

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:53.310
This release passed three hundred and seventy seven SQLite tests, three hundred and eighty three Postgres tests, and eighteen browser journeys.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.710
The database suites overlap.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:07:00.350
CI also checks types, lint, coverage, and builds.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:05.070
I reran the same large document benchmark around the search change.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:13.550
Local SQLite searched a ten mebibyte file in about twenty one milliseconds for a rare word and twenty five for a common phrase.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:17.350
Hosted Postgres is capped at five hundred thousand characters.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.190
The engine is only part of the cost.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:25.630
Indexes, revision snapshots, and returning the full document take more work.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:29.670
Full snapshots simplify restoring, but use more storage.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:34.270
I’d optimize from these measurements rather than assume the engine is the bottleneck.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:41.400
Here’s what I’d add for production: authentication, document permissions, and tenant isolation.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.520
I’d move larger snapshots into object storage and use paragraph chunks for search.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.373
Durable workers would handle large imports and AI jobs.

00:07:50.427 --> 00:07:54.480
This is a design proposal, not infrastructure I’ve already deployed.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:08:01.640
I’d monitor latency by document size, conflicts, search delay, queue age, and model spend.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.160
I’d test restoring backups and rolling back releases.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.200
Workspace budgets need durable enforcement.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.320
More services should earn their place through measurements.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.983
A few limits to be clear about.

00:08:14.037 --> 00:08:19.490
The public demo is shared, so it’s for fictional data, not confidential contracts.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:25.610
Editing is plain text, semantic review is advisory, and the real connectors need setup.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:29.450
I also went beyond the suggested time box while expanding the product.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:35.730
That’s Clause: precise edits, searchable content, and a reviewable history.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:43.410
The repo has setup steps, sample requests, tests, measurements, and the one page infrastructure proposal.

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.490
You can try the live demo too.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:46.970
Thanks for taking a look.

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.650
I’m happy to walk through the code and the trade-offs with you.
